-- title:
--   Invariance of a natural family under the Γₜ-orbit relation
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the Čerednik–Drinfeld frame is fixed as follows.
--
--   *Primes and level.* $r$ and $\bar r$ are primes with $\bar r \neq r$, and $N$ is a nonzero natural number divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$.
--
--   *Coefficient rings.* $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), $\pi$ is an irreducible element (`hπ`), $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (`hres`), and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero field which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Next, $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (written `Onr`) is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal O$-algebra equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $\pi$-adic completeness (`hOnr_complete`), maximality of the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_max`), the condition that every element satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), the condition that every monic polynomial of positive degree over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo that ideal for all $x$ (`hFr`). Finally $\mathrm{vdet} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a monoid homomorphism with $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hvdet`); thus $\mathrm{vdet}$ is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   *Indefinite side and moduli.* $a, b \in \mathbb Q$ are such that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite ($0 < a$ or $0 < b$) and its ramified finite places are exactly those above $r$ and $\bar r$ (`hB`), and $\Lambda$ is a maximal order in it (`hΛ`). A scheme $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ via $f$, together with an assignment `pt` of an $\mathcal X$-point over each base point to each fake elliptic curve with $\Gamma_0(N)$-structure over each ring $S$, is a coarse moduli space in the sense of `IsCoarseModuli` (`h𝒳`): the assignment is constant on isomorphism classes, compatible with base change, bijective on geometric points, and initial among such assignments.
--
--   *Fine level structure.* $n \geq 3$ is coprime to $N$ and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$; $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ via $f_M$, with `ptF` assigning points to fake elliptic curves carrying a full level-$n$ structure, is a fine moduli space (`hM`: invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, and bijectivity of `ptF` over every ring). A group $G$ acts on $M$ by $\rho$, with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$, and `hρ` asserts that this is a level-twist action: each $\rho(g)$ lies over the base, twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ moves the moduli point by $\rho(g)$, and $\chi$ is a bijection onto the units of $\Lambda/n\Lambda$ in the sense of the clauses `label_one`, `label_mul`, `label_surjective`, `label_injective`.
--
--   *Forgetful map, Hecke correspondences, Atkin–Lehner.* $p : M \to \mathcal X$ lies over the base ($p$ followed by $f$ is $f_M$) and is invariant under the $G$-action (`hρp`), and carries the fine moduli points to the coarse ones (`hp_pt`). For each prime $\ell$ distinct from $r$ and $\bar r$ (the type `HeckeTower.AwayPrime r rbar`), $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ via $g_\ell$ with `ptT` is a coarse moduli space for fake elliptic curves with extra level-$\ell$ structure (`h𝒴`), and $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ lie over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) and realise the two degeneracy maps: $d_0$ forgets the extra level structure (`hd₀`), and $d_1$ sends a point to the moduli point of any curve receiving the corresponding $\ell$-isogeny (`hd₁`). Endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ lie over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) and implement the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on moduli points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   *Definite side.* $a_1, b_1 \in \mathbb Q$ are such that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is definite ($a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$) and ramified exactly at the places above $\bar r$ (`hdef`); $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order, $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$; $n_1$ is a finite idèle lying in $\mathrm{primeHeckeSet}(R_1, r)$, and the intersection $\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1) = R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $N r$ (`hS₁`). Further, $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map, and $v$ is a height-one prime of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$. The subgroup $\Gamma_t$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ is characterised (`hΓt`) as the group `CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v` of units integral with respect to $R_1$ at every finite place other than $v$. Elements $s_\ell \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ and finite idèles $sf_\ell$ satisfy, for each $\ell$, the four clauses `hs`: the component of $sf_\ell$ at every place not containing $r$ is $s_\ell \otimes 1$; its component at places containing $r$ is $1$; the product of the diagonal image of the scalar $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in $\mathrm{levelHeckeUSet}(\Lambda_1, \mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1), \ell)$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in $\mathrm{primeHeckeSet}(\mathrm{meetOrder}(R_1,n_1), \ell)$ otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$. The subgroups $\Gamma_{t,\ell}$ are the intersections $\Gamma_t \cap s_\ell \Gamma_t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). An element $\bar w$ has reduced norm $\bar r$ and normalises $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`). A homomorphism $\theta_t : \Gamma_t \to G$ is given.
--
--   *The fine Čerednik–Drinfeld family.* Write $F$ for the functor $(\mathrm{corep}\,O^{\mathrm{nr}} \times \Omega_{K_0,\pi}) \times \underline{G}$ on $\mathcal O$-algebras, so that $F(B)$ consists of triples $((\psi, P), g)$ with $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, $P$ a Deligne datum over $B$ (a family of lines in the base changes of the full lattices in $K_0^2$, invertible quotients, monotone, homothety-invariant and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$) and $g \in G$. For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ becomes nilpotent, $\Theta_f$ maps $F(B)$ to the set of $B$-points of $M$ over the structural map, and it satisfies: naturality in $B$ (`hnat`); the equivariance $\rho(h) \circ \Theta_f((x, gh)) = \Theta_f((x,g))$ (`hG`); invariance under the twisted action, i.e. $\Theta_f((x', \theta_t(\gamma)g)) = \Theta_f((x,g))$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ and $x, x'$ satisfy `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` at $\iota_0(\gamma)$, meaning $x'$ has first component $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0\gamma)}$ and its Deligne datum is the pullback of that of $x$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$ (`hinv`); a formal-étaleness clause (`het`): along any surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map $B \to B_0$ of $\pi$-nilpotent algebras whose kernel has zero product, any $B$-point of $M$ lifting $\Theta_f$ of a given element of $F(B_0)$ comes from a unique element of $F(B)$ over it; and a geometric-points clause (`hgeom`): for every algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-field $k$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$, every $k$-point of $M$ is of the form $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g)$, and $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$ holds if and only if there is $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ with $g' = \theta_t(\gamma)g$, with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, and with $\psi'(y) = (\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0\gamma)})(y)$ for every $y \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0 z)}$ for all rational scalars $z \in \Gamma_t$ with $\theta_t(z) = 1$.
--
--   *The test family.* $T$ is a scheme over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ via $t$, and $\rho'$ assigns to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent a map $F(B) \to \{B\text{-points of } T \text{ over the base}\}$, natural in $B$ (`hρnat`) and invariant under the twisted action of $\Gamma_t$ in the same sense as `hinv`, namely $\rho'(x', \theta_t(\gamma)g) = \rho'(x,g)$ whenever $x,x'$ satisfy `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` at $\iota_0(\gamma)$ (`hρinv`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $C$ in which $\pi$ becomes nilpotent, all $x_1, x_2 \in F(C)$ and every $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$, the three conditions
--
--   (i) the Deligne datum $x_2.1.2$ is the pullback of $x_1.1.2$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, that is, for every full lattice $M \subseteq K_0^2$ the line of $x_2.1.2$ at $M$ is the preimage of the line of $x_1.1.2$ at $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}M$ under the base-changed action isomorphism;
--
--   (ii) for every $y \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0 z)}$ for all $z \in \Gamma_t$ that are rational multiples of $1$ and satisfy $\theta_t(z) = 1$, the $\mathcal O$-algebra maps satisfy $x_2.1.1(y) = \bigl(x_1.1.1 \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0\gamma)}\bigr)(y)$;
--
--   (iii) $x_2.2 = \theta_t(\gamma)\, x_1.2$ in $G$;
--
--   together imply $\rho'(C)(x_2) = \rho'(C)(x_1)$.
--
--   The hypothesis (ii) is weaker than the corresponding clause of `hρinv`, which demands equality of the two algebra maps on all of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$; the content of the theorem is that agreement on the invariants of the relevant powers of $\mathrm{Fr}$ suffices.
--
--   This is the invariance upgrade needed to descend the fine Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: it shows that a natural family $\rho'$ invariant under the twisted $\Gamma_t$-action is automatically invariant under the coarser equivalence relation that actually governs the geometric points of the uniformising functor, in which the Frobenius-twisted embedding of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is only required to agree on the fixed subring attached to the central elements of $\ker\theta_t$. It is used in the comparison theorem [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_eq_of_forall_frobFixed_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_eq_of_forall_frobFixed_eq) and in the factorisation statement [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.existsUnique_factor_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.existsUnique_factor_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine).
